# Zen Learner environment variables.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local dev; set the same names in your hosting
# provider's per-environment settings for a deploy. Placeholders only in this
# file -- it is committed, so never paste a real key here.
#
# Every variable below is optional. The app is designed to run with none of
# them set: the authored hint ladder is the real product and the tutor is a
# layer on top of it (docs/designs/zen-mode-vertical-slice.md, constraint #3).
# Deploy setup and the spend cap that actually bounds loss: docs/deploy.md


# --- The provider key -------------------------------------------------------

# Anthropic API key for the tutor route. Optional.
# Missing: app/api/tutor/route.ts:140 returns `ok: false, reason:
# "not-configured"` with a 200 and the authored hint. The pause still works,
# the learner sees a hand-written hint instead of a rewritten one, and nothing
# logs a warning. Also required by `pnpm eval:tutor`
# (scripts/eval-tutor.ts:94), which refuses to run without it.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-REPLACE-ME


# --- Model selection --------------------------------------------------------

# Which model the tutor calls. Optional.
# Missing: defaults to claude-haiku-4-5 (lib/tutor/config.ts:79-83).
# Only a model with a rate card in RATE_CARDS (lib/tutor/config.ts:35) is
# accepted; anything else makes rateCard() throw at request time
# (lib/tutor/config.ts:88), which the route's catch turns into a
# `provider-error` fallback. Valid today: claude-haiku-4-5, claude-sonnet-5.
# Leave this unset in production. See the cost note in docs/deploy.md before
# changing it -- sonnet-5 can exceed the $0.03-per-pause ceiling on a pause
# where every rung leaks and retries.
# ZEN_TUTOR_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5


# --- Convenience guards on /api/tutor ---------------------------------------
#
# These two are NOT authentication. The route's own comment says so
# (app/api/tutor/route.ts:62-68): the secret ships in the client bundle and is
# readable by anyone who opens devtools. They make casual misuse of the
# endpoint inconvenient. The provider spend cap is the real bound.

# The single origin allowed to call /api/tutor. Optional.
# Missing: app/api/tutor/route.ts:79 returns `true` and every origin is
# accepted, silently, with no log. Set it to the deployed origin exactly,
# scheme and host, no trailing slash.
# Note: a request with no Origin header passes even when this is set
# (app/api/tutor/route.ts:83), because a same-origin fetch omits the header on
# some browsers. A bare curl therefore is not a test of this guard.
# ZEN_TUTOR_ORIGIN=https://zen-learner.example

# Shared secret the route expects in the `x-zen-tutor` request header.
# Optional. Server-side name: not readable by the browser.
# Missing: app/api/tutor/route.ts:76 skips the check entirely (`if (secret &&
# ...)`), so the route accepts any caller, silently, with no log.
# Set this OR the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name below, not both -- the route reads
# ZEN_TUTOR_SHARED_SECRET first (app/api/tutor/route.ts:74-75), so setting two
# different values means the browser sends the wrong one and every real
# request 403s.
# ZEN_TUTOR_SHARED_SECRET=any-random-string

# The same secret under a name the browser can read.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is inlined into the client bundle at build time by Next.js, so
# this value is visible to anyone who loads the app. That is not a mistake --
# it is the only way the client can attach the header (lib/tutor/useTutor.ts:94)
# -- but it is exactly why the guard is a speed bump and not a lock.
# Missing: the client sends no `x-zen-tutor` header at all
# (lib/tutor/useTutor.ts:94-95). Harmless when no secret is configured
# server-side; if ZEN_TUTOR_SHARED_SECRET is set and this is not, every request
# from the browser gets a 403 and the pause falls to the authored ladder.
# This is a build-time value: changing it needs a rebuild, not a restart.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ZEN_TUTOR_SECRET=any-random-string


# --- Not yours to set -------------------------------------------------------
#
# NODE_ENV is managed by Next.js. Two behaviours hang off it: the per-request
# cost line only prints when it is "development" (app/api/tutor/route.ts:234),
# and `costUsd` is only included in the response body in development
# (app/api/tutor/route.ts:219). In production there is no cost logging at all,
# which is why the provider console is the only place spend is visible.
